ncy but not the district's lean"], "rationale": "The district's R+11 lean, a Republican-friendly national environment, and Alfonso's dominant fundraising and outside spending all point to a comfortable Republican hold, and every major rater calls it Safe or Solid R. Markets price Democratic chances at roughly 5 percent, consistent with a Safe R rating.", "watch": "Any late-breaking controversy around Alfonso's comments on raising the voting age or a strong Clark debate performance could tighten the race, though the district lean makes an upset unlikely.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Tom Tiffany (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tom Tiffany's retirement leaves Wisconsin's 7th District open, but the seat remains firmly Republican territory with an R+11 partisan lean. Michael Alfonso, a Duffy son-in-law, won a contested GOP primary and enters the general election with a major financial advantage, raising over $1.3 million and benefiting from more than $2.2 million in outside support. Fred Clark has raised a modest $423,000 with no reported outside spending.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and expert judgment. All nine ratings tracked call the race Safe or Solid R, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a 5 percent chance. Alfonso has skipped some debate appearances, drawing criticism from Clark, but nothing suggests the district's underlying lean is in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0554, "margin": -10.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0396, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -13.0}]}